From f3d66d44f2898a7c807dbecc860e2992d8dc2d27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wjxn13 <yqyj33@qq.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 22:31:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] fix(core): strip ACP hook-block wrapper instead of
 silencing the whole reply

On ACP-backed agents (CodeBuddy CLI / DSH) the hook-block notice of one turn is
carried into the next turn's reply and concatenated with it, with no separator:

  Operation stopped by hook: NO_REPLY<real reply>

The previous strategy matched that prefix and silenced the entire response, so a
real reply got swallowed. Reproduced live 2/2 before this fix:

  turn A (blocked)   tools=0 response_len=35  silent=true
  turn B (poisoned)  tools=2 response_len=270 silent=true   <- real reply lost

Fix: for the ACP shape only, strip "Operation stopped by hook: " plus the
NO_REPLY reason marker, then decide silence on what remains:

  remaining empty      -> silent (pure block; behaviour unchanged)
  remaining non-empty  -> deliver the real reply

Order matters: strip first, then check. The Claude Code shape
("UserPromptSubmit operation blocked by hook: ...") is deliberately NOT stripped
- that text is entirely wrapper - so it still falls through to
isHookBlockedResponse and is silenced as before.

Caveat: changing the hook's reason text away from NO_REPLY disables detection;
core/hook_block_silence_test.go pins that with a dedicated case.

Coverage added:
- TestStripHookBlockWrapper (7 cases)
- TestProcessInteractiveEvents_StaleWrapperDoesNotEatReply
- "acp-pure-block-no-reply" added to the silence table

Reverse control: short-circuiting stripHookBlockWrapper makes both new tests
fail (silent=true, nothing delivered), so they carry real discriminating power.

Live end-to-end re-run after deploying the patched binary (three consecutive
turns on the same session):

  A (blocked)   tools=0 response_len=35 silent=true    suppressed
  B (poisoned)  tools=2 response_len=46 silent=false   delivered; the 35-byte
                                                       wrapper was stripped
  C (clean)     tools=2 response_len=37 silent=false   untouched

The B length is the arithmetic proof: 46 == len("B<confirm text>"), i.e. exactly
the text left after removing the 35-byte wrapper (35 + 46 == 81 would be the
unstripped length).
